    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you go into a bit more detail on the molecular mechanism of how Leucine actually interacts with the mTOR complex? Specifically, how does it sense intracellular leucine levels to trigger that response? I want to be able to explain the \"switch\" to my client.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Leucine primarily signals through the Rag GTPases, which are key regulators of mTORC1. When cellular leucine levels are high, leucine activates specific sensors that recruit and activate the Rag complexes. This activation shifts the position of mTORC1 on the lysosomal surface, allowing it to associate with and be stimulated by Rheb, which acts as the final direct activator of the complex. This mechanism essentially functions like a high-level biological switch, only flipping \"on\" when sufficient leucine is available to signal adequate amino acid availability for growth.",
      "source": "target",
