    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Got it, so the lysosomal surface is basically the staging area for the activation. On that note, when we're talking about the metabolic cost of this process—if the client isn't hitting enough leucine or their calories are too low, how does that failure to trigger the pathway look on a cellular level? Like, what is actually happening to those signaling processes when the \"switch\" stays off?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When the mTOR pathway remains suppressed due to low leucine or inadequate energy, the signaling cascade stalls at the initiation points. Specifically, downstream targets like S6K1 and 4E-BP1 remain inactive or improperly regulated, preventing them from driving ribosome biogenesis and initiating protein translation effectively. At a cellular level, this results in decreased rates of mRNA translation into functional muscle proteins, leading to a net catabolic state where breakdown can exceed synthesis.",
      "source": "target",
